Subject: [overlay 0029] VR HUD Size, and the weapon's scale pivot re-anchored to the fist

The second headset verdict, items 8 and 9. Three additive seams, all inert on
desktop (no caller outside the visionOS shell), all under the q2vr gate:

  * shared.h    — `vm_pivot`, APPENDED. The point Weapon Size scales about, written by
                  the engine from the origin it actually gave the entity.
  * client.h /
    screen.c    — SCR_HudScaleChanged takes a MULTIPLIER (the HUD Size row). Its only
                  caller is the shell's 2D-redirect bracket, which passes 1.0 outside it.
  * entities.c  — the grip correction is multiplied by the weapon scale, so the gun's
                  grip point is the pivot at every size instead of drifting out of the
                  hand by (s-1)*|grip| — and the pivot is read back rather than predicted.

patch -p1 -d vendor/q2repro

--- a/inc/shared/shared.h
+++ b/inc/shared/shared.h
@@ -1406,6 +1406,11 @@
                                 // instrument subtracts the two to recover what the camera
                                 // actually did. NOT for the viewmodel mount: CL_AddEntities
                                 // runs BEFORE that block, so a reader there gets last frame's.
+
+    // [vr overlay R7b] APPENDED, never inserted, for the fifth time.
+    float   vm_pivot[3];        // WRITTEN BY THE ENGINE: the point Weapon Size scales ABOUT,
+                                // recovered from the origin the entity was actually given
+                                // rather than predicted from the inputs. See view.c.
 } q2_vr_state_t;
 
 extern q2_vr_state_t q2vr;
--- a/inc/client/client.h
+++ b/inc/client/client.h
@@ -95,7 +95,7 @@
 void SCR_EndLoadingPlaque(void);
 
 void SCR_ModeChanged(void);
-void SCR_HudScaleChanged(void);     // [overlay 0026] HUD scale only — see screen.c
+void SCR_HudScaleChanged(float mul);   // [overlay 0026/0029] HUD scale only — see screen.c
 void SCR_UpdateScreen(void);
 
 // stats system
--- a/src/client/screen.c
+++ b/src/client/screen.c
@@ -1214,10 +1214,30 @@
 // routed to a different surface entirely, so the only 2D inside the bracket is the HUD, whose
 // layout depends on r_config and this scale and nothing else. Unused off that path — no
 // caller on desktop, so the reference build's behaviour is byte-identical.
-void SCR_HudScaleChanged(void)
+// [ios port overlay R7b] `mul` is the VR HUD Size row: a multiple of whatever scale the
+// engine would have chosen on its own. The second headset verdict was "HUD is WAY too
+// small", and the reason is arithmetic rather than taste — the auto scale is picked from the
+// VIEWPORT's pixel count (get_auto_scale in draw.c), and the HUD's 16:9 sub-rect of a
+// supersampled eye texture is a big viewport that subtends a small angle. A number that is
+// right for a monitor is wrong for a quad 1.75 m from your face.
+//
+// It is applied HERE and not as a transform on the finished quad, which is the road the Q3
+// port took. Its HUD is carved into three world-space bands whose source rects it cannot
+// re-lay-out, so scaling the quads was the only move available; this port composes one HUD
+// into one sub-rect, so it can re-derive the scale and have the engine LAY THE HUD OUT
+// LARGER. The difference is visible: a magnified texture on a surface that is already
+// resampled twice on its way to the eye is bigger and blurrier, and blurrier is the half
+// that a headset punishes.
+//
+// `hud_scale` is a RECIPROCAL (R_SetScale orthos `r_config.width * scale`), so a bigger HUD
+// is a SMALLER number — dividing is the multiply. Guarded, because a zero here would ortho
+// the 2D layer to nothing and take the HUD, the crosshair and centerprint with it.
+void SCR_HudScaleChanged(float mul)
 {
+    if (mul < 0.05f)
+        mul = 1.0f;
     if (scr.initialized)
-        scr.hud_scale = R_ClampScale(scr_scale);
+        scr.hud_scale = R_ClampScale(scr_scale) / mul;
     // [ios port overlay R7a] AND THE EXTENTS, which 0026 left behind — the defect that
     // clipped the weapon wheel into the top-right quadrant (Q-VR8 item 5).
     //
--- a/src/client/entities.c
+++ b/src/client/entities.c
@@ -1353,9 +1353,45 @@
         gun.angles[YAW]   = q2vr.hand_ang[YAW] + sy;
         gun.angles[ROLL]  = q2vr.hand_ang[ROLL];
         AngleVectors(gun.angles, hf, hr, hu);
-        VectorMA(gun.origin, q2vr.grip_ofs[0], hf, gun.origin);
-        VectorMA(gun.origin, q2vr.grip_ofs[1], hr, gun.origin);
-        VectorMA(gun.origin, q2vr.grip_ofs[2], hu, gun.origin);
+        // [vr overlay R7b] THE GRIP CORRECTION SCALES WITH THE WEAPON, and that one factor is
+        // the whole of Q-VR8 item 9's second half ("anchored so Weapon Size scaling stays
+        // location-locked; no re-tuning offsets per size").
+        //
+        // R4's comment above claims Weapon Size "comes for free, and correctly, because the
+        // renderer scales an entity about its ORIGIN and the origin is now the grip point".
+        // The first half is true; the second is not. The renderer scales about the MODEL's own
+        // local origin, and `grip_ofs` is precisely the vector that moves that local origin
+        // AWAY from the fist — it is where the model's origin has to sit for the part the hand
+        // is holding to land in the hand. Call that offset g. The model point that sits in the
+        // fist is at -g in model space, so at scale s it renders at `origin - s*R*g`; with
+        // `origin` fixed at `fist + R*g` the gun slides (s-1)*|g| out of the hand — about six
+        // units per doubling with the shipped -6 forward. The player would have had to re-dial
+        // three offsets after every drag of the size slider.
+        //
+        // Wanted: fist == origin - s*R*g for every s. Therefore origin = fist + s*R*g. One
+        // factor. The pivot is then the fist at every size, which is what makes the claim
+        // testable rather than argued.
+        //
+        // (The Q3 port reaches the same place by a far longer road — it MEASURES an anchor per
+        // frame, because its cgame hangs each weapon off a `tag_weapon` with a 20-unit spread
+        // and the model bounds vary anti-correlated with it. Here the anchor is not measured:
+        // `grip_ofs` is already the answer to exactly the question that search is asking.)
+        {
+            const float gs = (q2vr.weapon_scale > 0.05f) ? q2vr.weapon_scale : 1.0f;
+            VectorMA(gun.origin, q2vr.grip_ofs[0] * gs, hf, gun.origin);
+            VectorMA(gun.origin, q2vr.grip_ofs[1] * gs, hr, gun.origin);
+            VectorMA(gun.origin, q2vr.grip_ofs[2] * gs, hu, gun.origin);
+
+            // READ BACK OFF THE FINISHED MOUNT, never predicted from the inputs: subtract the
+            // scaled correction from the origin the entity is ACTUALLY being given. A build
+            // that scaled about the wrong point then reports a pivot that MOVES with the size
+            // slider, and the assertion is simply "this does not move". Recomputing it from
+            // `hand_ofs` would restate the arithmetic under test and pass either way.
+            VectorCopy(gun.origin, q2vr.vm_pivot);
+            VectorMA(q2vr.vm_pivot, -q2vr.grip_ofs[0] * gs, hf, q2vr.vm_pivot);
+            VectorMA(q2vr.vm_pivot, -q2vr.grip_ofs[1] * gs, hr, q2vr.vm_pivot);
+            VectorMA(q2vr.vm_pivot, -q2vr.grip_ofs[2] * gs, hu, q2vr.vm_pivot);
+        }
 
         VectorCopy(gun.origin, gun.oldorigin);      // re-taken: the origin moved under it
         if (q2vr.weapon_scale > 0.05f && q2vr.weapon_scale != 1.0f)
